module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.annotation_inside_a_declaration_body_is_invisible_to_the_authoring_gate

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data annotation_inside_a_declaration_body_is_invisible_to_the_authoring_gate: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "annotation_inside_a_declaration_body_is_invisible_to_the_authoring_gate" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a `//` annotation block indented INSIDE a declaration body -- most often inside a list literal, beside the row it explains. DESIGN section 4c admits annotations only as standalone leading blocks attached to MODULE-SCOPE declarations; body, trailing and unattached forms refuse. The refusal is one blocking error PER LINE, so a six-line explanation is six blocking errors, and the diagnostic is exact: `source annotation sits inside a declaration body. Only module-item grain is modeled; move it above the declaration it describes`.",
    "HARM IS NOT THE REFUSAL, IT IS WHERE THE REFUSAL IS NOT. The state is loud at the compile gate and SILENT on the path an author actually uses: `gunbc run --entry` does not perform the section 4c check. So the edit is made, the witness is run, the witness returns true, and the author has executed evidence that the change works while the file carries blocking errors. The check that would catch it is not the check the work naturally reaches for.",
    "THE DISTINGUISHING FACT, which is what makes this predictable rather than anecdotal: it appears when EDITING A LIST BODY. That is where an author most wants to explain a single row -- why this entry is enrolled, why that one was removed -- and the natural place for the explanation is beside the row, which is inside the declaration. Every instance below is a list literal. A comment ABOUT a declaration goes above it and is fine; a comment about one ELEMENT has no admitted position at all, and that absence is the trap.",
    "RECEIPT ONE, gunbc#11311, std.decision. A four-line block explaining why the decision scope is minted before the outcome is known sat inside `select_realization`'s body. Four blocking errors. This one was caught by a compile before it was pushed, and it is the only reason the other two were found at all.",
    "RECEIPT TWO AND THREE, gunbc#11193, same afternoon, both in one commit: a six-line block inside the `v4_1_selection_frontier` list body explaining why one frontier row lives in that module, and a five-line block inside `v4_1_expected_frontier_subjects` explaining why a subject is enrolled there. Eleven blocking errors, in a head that had been REPORTED AS GREEN on the strength of two roster witnesses that returned true over those exact two files.",
    "AND THE REPORTING FAILURE THAT SHIPPED IT IS PART OF THE CLASS. The author had ALSO verified `zero blocking errors in any file this PR owns` -- a true reading of a real whole-tree gate run taken at an EARLIER SHA, before the commit that introduced the comments. So both instruments agreed and both were answering about something other than the tree being shipped: one does not perform this check, the other performed it on a different tree. A gate verdict is a property of the tree it read; it does not follow the branch forward.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The invalid state is fully writable, the refusal is typed and located and names the remedy, and containment depends entirely on a compile running before the change is believed. Nothing prevents authoring it and nothing on the authoring path reports it.",
    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, and deliberately not higher. The state is decidable -- annotation position relative to declaration boundaries is in the parse -- but it is not structurally impossible while `//` remains writable anywhere in the file, and DESIGN section 4c keeps `//` as the explicit quarantine boundary for exactly that reason. Making the position unwritable would mean a comment syntax the language does not have; the honest ceiling is that every path which accepts source performs the check.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY RATHER THAN AS AN ARTIFACT: the run-entry path performs the section 4c annotation check on the sources it loads, so the authoring path and the acceptance path stop disagreeing about whether a file is admissible. A lint, a reviewer habit, or an author remembering to compile does not retire this row -- each leaves the disagreement in place and depends on someone choosing the second instrument.",
    "WHAT IS EXPLICITLY NOT THIS CLASS: `extra_source_root_outside_workspace_refuses_annotations_as_unattached` is a DIFFERENT failure with an opposite shape -- there the annotation is correctly placed and the refusal is spurious, caused by a source root outside the process workspace, and the harm is a diagnostic naming the wrong subject. Here the refusal is CORRECT and the harm is that the path an author uses never issues it.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
